What is DocumentLink? Our DocumentLink feature allows you to send invoices to your customers for payment over the web. DocumentLink is a feature available in our Payments Premium Plan. 
 Main Use Case:  Send customers a DocumentLink URL to view an itemized invoice and pay. 
 Quick Video:  Check out the overview video below to see it in action!  &#128249; 
 Quick Tip:  You can use the "Amount to Pay" field to capture multiple payments against one invoice.  &#128184;

What are we targeting? Depending on your use case, you may want to remove the "Welcome Banner" that shows up when Attendees click on the AttendeeLink from your invitation email on your Dynamic UI events. Here is the banner we are referring to: 

 CSS You can use custom CSS to remove the banner. Below is the CSS you will want to use to accomplish this: 

 .welcome.sticky {
 display: none !important;
}


 After applying the CSS code it should look like this as the CSS is configured to simply remove the banner all altogether:

&#128184; Refunds: Keeping Things Smooth for You and Your Attendees Let?s be real?plans change. Life happens. And sometimes, that means an attendee needs a refund. The good news? Blackthorn Events makes the refund process  super simple , so you can take care of it with just a few clicks. &#127881; 
 So, whether someone accidentally double-booked or their plans took a turn, you?ve got the tools to handle refunds quickly and easily. Let?s walk through how it works! &#128071; &#128260;  Processing a Refund: Easy as 1, 2, 3! If you ever need to refund a payment, you?ll do it right from the  Transaction Record  in Salesforce. Here?s how: ?  Full Refund ? Find the  Transaction Record  for the payment in Salesforce. ? Click  Refund  (yep, that?s it!). ? Confirm the refund, and you?re done! &#127881; 
 ??  Partial Refund  (because sometimes you just need to return part of a payment) ? Find the  Transaction Record  for the payment. ? Click  Refund  and enter the specific amount you want to return. ? Confirm, and Blackthorn will process the refund for you! &#128161;  Pro Tip:  Refund amounts  can?t  be more than the original payment (I am sure you?d love to send out bonus money, but that?s not how finance works! ). &#127974;  What About My Payment Gateway? While Blackthorn makes refunds simple, each payment gateway (the system that actually moves the money) has its own rules. Let?s take a look at what you need to know: &#127974;  Stripe You're good to go, the Stripe/Blackthorn integration handles Refunds without any issues! &#128179;  Authorize.net The user processing the refund  must  have the "Create refund transactions" permission enabled in Authorize.net. If you run into any issues, double-check that setting! &#128274;  Spreedly (if your gateway connects through Spreedly) Spreedly itself doesn?t decide refund rules?the payment gateway you connected through Spreedly does. Before processing a refund, check your gateway?s refund policies to make sure everything is good to go! &#128161;  Not sure about your payment gateway?s refund rules?  No worries! You can always check Blackthorn?s  support documentation  or contact your Payment Gateway's support team for help. &#128680;  Heads-Up: A Few Things to Keep in Mind &#128172;  Disputes & Chargebacks:  If a payment is being disputed (like if the attendee contacted their bank to challenge the charge), you  won?t  be able to issue a refund until the dispute is resolved. These can take up to 75 days, so hang tight! 
 &#128679;  Salesforce Rules & Restrictions:  If you have validation rules in your Salesforce setup that limit how transactions are processed, just double-check they?re not blocking refunds. 
 &#128064;  Double Payments?  Be careful when processing refunds to avoid duplicate transactions! Always check the transaction record before clicking "Refund" just to be sure. 
 &#128274;  Restricting Refund Access:  Since refunds in Blackthorn are  so simple  to process, you may want to  restrict who can see the refund button or process refunds . You can manage this by  adjusting user permissions  in Salesforce to limit access to only approved users! &#127881; That?s It! Refunds Are Handled! And there you have it?refunds in Blackthorn Events are designed to be as smooth and simple as possible. Whether you?re handling a full refund or just returning part of a payment, you?re in control. &#128588; &#128284;  What?s Next? DocumentLink?because once attendees pay for their event, they might need invoices or receipts to keep everything organized! &#128196; &#128184; ?? Let?s dive into DocumentLink! &#128640;

&#129534;  Write-Offs & Allocations: Managing Event Payments with Ease Let?s talk about  Jessica. 
 Jessica is an important  VIP attendee  at your event?maybe she?s a keynote speaker, an industry partner, or someone you?ve invited as a special guest. Normally, she would have been charged  $500  for her registration, but because of her  VIP status , your organization has decided  not to collect that payment . 
 Since Jessica  was originally invoiced , but you?re choosing to  waive the fee , you need to  clear her balance  while keeping your financial records accurate. This is a perfect case for a  Write-Off!  &#127881; 
 Or maybe another scenario:  Jessica?s company originally registered two employees but later decided to swap one for a different event.  Instead of refunding or manually adjusting balances, you  apply their original payment toward the new invoice . That?s where  Allocations  come in! 
 Let?s walk through  how to process a Write-Off or Allocation  for Jessica. &#128640; ??  Scenario 1: Writing Off Jessica?s Registration Fee as a VIP Since you?re  waiving Jessica?s registration fee , you need to  write off her invoice balance  so it no longer appears as an unpaid amount in your system. &#128205;  To process a Write-Off: 1??  Find Jessica?s Invoice  ? Navigate to the  Invoices  tab and open the invoice for her registration. 2??  Create a Write-Off Transaction  ? Click  New Transaction , set  Transaction Type  to  Write-Off , and enter the full amount ($500) to remove. 3??  Save & Confirm  ? The system will automatically adjust the balance and  close out the invoice . &#127881; 
 Now, Jessica?s invoice  shows a $0 balance , and your reports  stay accurate  while properly reflecting that she was comped as a VIP. ? &#128260;  Scenario 2: Allocating a Payment Toward a Different Event Instead of a write-off, let?s say Jessica?s company  initially paid for two employees to attend your event  but later decided  one person will attend a future event instead . Rather than issuing a refund and having them pay again, you can  allocate the existing payment  to a different event. &#128205;  To allocate a payment: 1??  Go to Transactions  ? Find the  original payment transaction  in Blackthorn. 2??  Create an Allocation  ? Click  New Allocation  and select  where the extra amount should be applied  (a different invoice or event item). 3??  Save & Confirm  ? Blackthorn will  automatically adjust the balances  across the linked invoices. &#127919; 
 Now, their payment  seamlessly moves to the correct invoice  without additional steps! &#128736;?  Making This Even Easier with Automation If you regularly deal with  VIP write-offs, payment adjustments, or reallocations , you can set  Blackthorn to automatically create Allocations  when invoices are generated. &#128205;  To enable automatic allocations: 1?? Go to  Setup > Custom Settings > Blackthorn | Payment Trigger Settings . 2?? Click  Manage  and set  Automatically Create Allocation  to  True . 3?? Click  Save ?now, allocations will  process automatically!  &#128640; &#128640;  Best Practices for Write-Offs & Allocations ?  Use Write-Offs for VIP attendees  instead of manually adjusting balances. ?  Use Allocations to track payments  instead of issuing refunds. ?  Review invoices regularly  to catch any adjustments early. ?  Enable automation  to streamline financial updates and reduce manual work. &#128284;  What?s Next? Now that we?ve taken care of  adjustments, write-offs, and payment reallocations , let?s  wrap up Paid Events  by looking at  Payouts ?because tracking when your money  actually hits your account  is just as important as collecting it! &#128176;
